==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|la|:inh|itc-pro:*fars}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*fars|t=flour, grain}},<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|pages=201-2}}</ref> possibly from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*bʰers-||spike, prickle}}; compare {{cog|cy|bara||bread}}, {{cog|en|barley}}, {{cog|sh|brašno|brȁšno|flour}}, {{cog|sq|bar||grass}}, {{cog|grc|Φηρῶν||plant deity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|far}}
* {{la-IPA|far.r|eccl=no}} ''(before a vowel)''

The nominative-accusative singular form scans as a long syllable in Ovid (cited below). Therefore, some sources mark the vowel in this form as long (''fār''), but an alternative explanation is that despite being spelled with a single letter r, this word form was pronounced with the underlying geminate {{IPAchar|/rr/}} of the stem when the following word started with a vowel.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Charles Edwin Bennett|title=The Latin Language: A Historical Outline of Its Sounds, Inflections, and Syntax|year=1907|page=118|pageurl=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Latin_Language/N1RfAAAAMAAJ?q=farr&gbpv=1&bsq=farr}}</ref>

===Noun===
{{la-noun|far/farr<3.N>}}

# [[farro]], a type of hulled wheat. {{gloss|Most likely [[emmer]] (''Triticum dicoccum'' or {{taxlink|Triticum turgidum subsp. dicoccon|subspecies}}) but often mistranslated as [[spelt]]  ({{taxfmt|Triticum spelta|species}})}}  <ref>[https://www.jstor.org/stable/703297 Thompson, D'Arcy W. “Wheat in Antiquity.” The Classical Review, vol. 60, no. 3, 1946, pp. 120–122. JSTOR. Accessed 6 June 2021.]</ref>  <ref>[https://www.eruditio-antiqua.mom.fr/vol6/EA6k.Glinister.pdf Glinister, Fay “Festus and Ritual Foodstuffs.” Eruditio Antiqua 6 (2014), pp. 215-227.]</ref>
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|1|338|quote= Ante, deos homini quod conciliare valeret, / '''far''' erat et puri lucida mica salis. |trans=Of old, the means to win the goodwill of the gods were '''far''' and sparkling grains of pure salt.}}<small>― Fay Glinister, “[https://www.eruditio-antiqua.mom.fr/vol6/EA6k.Glinister.pdf Festus and Ritual Foodstuffs]” p. 220</small>
