From {{inh|en|enm|faste}}, {{m|enm|fasten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fæsten|t=fast, fasting}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*fastuni}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*fastu(b)nją|t=fast, fasting}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|festene}}, {{m|ofs|feste}} (Modern {{cog|fy|fêsten}}, {{m|fy|fêste}}), {{cog|dum|vastene}} (Modern {{cog|nl|vasten}}), {{cog|gml|vastene}} (Modern {{cog|nds-de|Fasten}}), {{cog|de|Faste}}, {{m|de|Fasten|t=fast, fasting}}, {{cog|got|𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹|t=fasting}}. Possibly merged with {{der|en|non|fasta|t=fast, fasting}}, whence also {{cog|da|faste|t=fast, fasting}}, {{cog|sv|fasta|t=fast, fasting}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The act or practice of [[fasting]], religious abstinence from food.
#* '''1677''' George Fox, ''The Hypocrites Fast and Feast Not God's Holy Day'', p. 8 (paraphrasing Matthew 6:16-18).
#*: And is it not the Command of Christ, that in their '''Fast''' they should not appear unto men to fast?
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1878|author=Joseph Bingham|title=The Antiquities of the Christian Church|volume=2|page=1182
|passage=anciently a change of diet was not reckoned a '''fast'''; but it consisted in a perfect abstinence from all sustenance for the whole day till evening.}}
# Any of the fasting periods in the liturgical year.
#* '''1662''' Peter Gunning, ''The Holy Fast of Lent Defended Against All Its Prophaners: Or, a Discourse, Shewing that Lent-Fast was First Taught the World by the Apostles'' (1677 [1662]), p. 13 (translation of the Paschal Epistle of Theophilus of Alexandria).
