# {{lb|en|mathematics}} The [[number]] by which a [[value]] (called the [[base]]) is said to be [[raise]]d to a [[power]] in [[exponentiation]]: for example, the <math>3</math> in <math>2^3=8</math>.
#: {{syn|en|power}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics|obsolete}} The [[degree]] to which the [[root]] of a [[radicand]] is found, for example, the <math>2</math> in <math>\sqrt[2]r=b</math>.
#: {{syn|en|degree|power}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Jacques Ozanam|[Jacques Ozanam]]]|tlr=Daniel Hilman|chapter=Abridgement of Algebra. Chapter I. Of Monomes.|title=M. Ozanam's Introduction to the Mathematicks or His Algebra: Wherein the Rudiments of that Most Useful Science are Made Plain to a Mean Capacity. Done out of French|location=London|publisher=Printed for R. Sare at [[w:Gray's Inn|Gray's-Inn-Gate]] in {{w|Holborn}}|year=1711|section=problem IV (“To Divide a Quantity by a Quantity”)|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bjY4AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA9|oclc=23617497|passage=A Power that hath neither the Signs <math>+</math> or <math>-</math> before it, is look'd upon as Affirmative, and if it be preceded by a Number that contains the Root ſought and its '''Exponent''' may be commenſured by the '''Exponent''' of the Root; namely for the Square Root by 2, for the Cube by 3, &c. it will contain the Root ſought.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Philip Ronayne|chapter=Of the Indices, or Exponents of Powers|title=A Treatise of Algebra in Two Books: The First Treating of the Arithmetical, and the Second of the Geometrical Part|location=London|publisher=Printed for W[illiam] Innys at the Prince's Arms in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-Yard]]|year=1717|volume_plain=book I, part V|page=69|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gc82AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA69|oclc=83267734|passage=And univerſally the '''Exponent''' of the ''m'' Power, is ''m'' times the '''Exponent''' of the Root, and the '''Exponent''' of the ''m''-Root (or <math>\frac{1}{m}</math> Power) is <math>\frac{1}{m}</math> times the '''Exponent''' of the Root.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Dionysius Lardner|chapter=Algebra|editors=w:Edward Smedley; w:Hugh James Rose; [[w:Henry Rose (priest)|Henry John Rose]]|title=[[w:Encyclopædia Metropolitana|Encyclopædia Metropolitana; or, Universal Dictionary of Knowledge, on an Original Plan: Comprising the Twofold Advantage of a Philosophical and an Alphabetical Arrangement, with Appropriate Engravings]]|location=London|publisher=B. Fellowes [''et al.'']|year=1845|volume=I (Pure Sciences, volume 1)|page=534|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EYXa49tpya4C&pg=PA534|oclc=20598255|passage=The notation by which the root is expressed, is the mark <math>\sqrt{}</math> called a radical, placed over the letter, with an '''exponent''' to the left indicating the order of the root.}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} A [[phonological]] manifestation of a [[morphosyntactic]] property; in layman's terms, the expression of one or more [[grammatical]] properties by sound.
#* {{quote-book|en|2015|Ruth Kramer|The Morphosyntax of Gender||83|However, there have been no examples presented of gender systems where the plain ''n'' triggers one '''exponent''' for gender agreement, and the male and female ''ns'' together trigger a different '''exponent'''.}}
