===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=exhaustion|:bor|fr:fatigue<id:tiredness>|tree=1}}
From {{bor|en|fr|fatigue}}, from {{m|fr|fatiguer}}, from {{der|en|la|fatīgō|fatīgāre|to weary, tire, vex, harass}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[weariness]] caused by [[exertion]]; [[exhaustion]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=181|passage=My husband stayed for some days with the magistrate at Cardwell, recruiting his health and recovering from his '''fatigues''', for the passage between Cape York and Cardwell had proved the most tedious and anxious part of the voyage.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 29, 2012|author=Paul Doyle| title=Arsenal's {{w|Theo Walcott}} hits hat-trick in thrilling victory over Newcastle| work=The Guardian| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2012/dec/29/arsenal-newcastle-united-premier-league| passage={{w|Alan Pardew}} finished by far the most frustrated man at the {{w|Emirates Stadium|Emirates}}, blaming '''fatigue''' for the fact that Arsenal were able to kill his team off in the dying minutes.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013073237/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/dec/29/arsenal-newcastle-united-premier-league|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Matt Stevens|title=Taylor Swift Has Given Fans a Lot. Is It Finally Too Much?|work=The New York Times|date=2024-04-22|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/22/arts/music/taylor-swift-album-fatigue.html|issn=0362-4331|author2=Shivani Gonzalez|passage=For some, the constant deluge that has peaked in the past year is starting to add up to a new (and previously unthinkable) feeling: Taylor Swift '''fatigue'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250902013719/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/22/arts/music/taylor-swift-album-fatigue.html|archivedate=2 September 2025}}
#* {{quote-text|en
 | passage = We found that a comprehensive '''fatigue''' management program can reduce '''fatigue'''-related crash risk and improve safety by, for example, educating drivers; had Triton had such a program, the driver may not have been operating the truck while fatigued. 
 | title = ''NTSB Finds Multiple Safety Lapses in 2022 Fatal Collision Between Truck and Party Bus''| author=NTSB| year= 2024
}}
# {{lb|en|often|in plural}} A menial task or tasks, especially in the military.
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=112|volume=I|passage=Moreover, the habits of business are the most enduring of any; and Lord Norbourne's most positive enjoyment was in what are called the '''fatigues''' of office.}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} Weakening and eventual [[failure]] of [[material]], typically by [[cracking]] leading to complete [[separation]], caused by repeated application of mechanical [[stress]] to the material.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=N. Dowling|title=Mechanical Behaviour of Materials|page=399
|passage=Mechanical failures due to '''fatigue''' have been the subject of engineering efforts for more than 150 years.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 March 18|author=Peter Plisner|title=Tackling broken rails: the skills and technology improving safety|journal=RAIL|issue=1057|page=30|text=An investigation found that the rail had fragmented as trains passed over it, and that the likely cause was "rolling contact '''fatigue'''" defined as multiple surface-breaking cracks. Repeated high loading had caused '''fatigue''' cracks to grow, resulting in what is called gauge corner cracking.}} 
# {{lb|en|US}} {{attributive form of|en|fatigues|t=[[military]] [[clothing]] worn when doing menial tasks}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Crowther|title=Firebase|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year=1975|page=107|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/firebase00crow/page/107/mode/1up|passage=He was slouched in the chair behind the duty officer’s desk, cigarette dangling from his lips, hands thrust deep into his '''fatigue''' pockets, making a display of his disrespect. The acting clerk, cowering behind his own desk, was either afraid to insist he assume a more military posture, or else didn’t care.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William T. Craig|title=Team Sergeant: A Special Forces NCO at Lang Vei and Beyond|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Ivy Books|year=1998|page=27|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/teamsergeantspec0000crai/page/27/mode/1up|isbn=0-8041-1714-4|passage=He reluctantly took the map from his camouflage '''fatigue''' pocket.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gavin G. Smith|title=Crysis: Escalation|location=London|publisher=[[w:Victor Gollancz Ltd|Gollancz]]|year=2013|page=186|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/crysisescalation0000smit/page/186/mode/1up|isbn=978 0 575 11570 5|passage=Chino took a laminated map out of one of his '''fatigue''' pockets and gave it to Harper.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Dalton Fury|title=Full Assault Mode|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year=2014|page=139|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fullassaultmode0000fury/page/139/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-250-04048-0|passage=Spencer came with all the soft-skill attributes of a desk officer. Double chin, bulging belly testing the tensile strength of the lower two buttons of his '''fatigue''' top, and wired-rimmed glasses that sat atop a pointed nose with mismatched nostrils.}}

